# SECTION 2: TEMPERATURE
# ==================================================================

upsc.append({"q":"Which one of the following statements about the temperature characteristics of the equatorial climate is NOT correct?","o":["The diurnal range of temperature is typically greater than the annual range","Mean monthly temperatures remain around 27°C throughout the year with very little variation","Regular land and sea breezes assist in maintaining a truly equable climate","Cloudiness and heavy precipitation help moderate the daily temperature, making the climate bearable even at the equator itself"],"a":0,"e":"This statement is incorrect — the text states both the diurnal range AND the annual range of temperature are small in the equatorial climate; it does not claim the diurnal range exceeds the annual range. The other three statements are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements comparing Kuala Lumpur (a lowland equatorial station) and Bogota (a highland equatorial station), per Goh Cheng Leong:\n1. Kuala Lumpur's annual temperature range is about 1°C, with its hottest month around 27°C and coolest month around 26°C.\n2. Bogota's mean monthly temperatures are comparatively lower than Kuala Lumpur's mainly because of its high altitude (about 2,850 metres above sea-level).\n3. Despite the altitude difference, Bogota's annual temperature range is also small, similar in magnitude to Kuala Lumpur's.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1, 2 and 3","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only"],"a":0,"e":"All three statements are correct, matching the text's comparison of a lowland station (Kuala Lumpur) and highland station (Bogota) within the equatorial climate, both showing the characteristic uniformity/small range of temperature despite the large difference in absolute temperature caused by altitude."})

upsc.append({"q":"How many of the following stations are cited by Goh Cheng Leong as recording a particularly small annual range of temperature characteristic of the equatorial climate?\n1. Singapore\n2. Jakarta\n3. Quito\n4. Colombo\n5. Jaluit (Marshall Islands)","o":["All five","Only two","Only three","Only four"],"a":0,"e":"All five stations (Singapore, Jakarta, Quito, Colombo, and Jaluit in the Marshall Islands) are cited in the text as examples of equatorial stations with a very small annual temperature range, with Jaluit — over the ocean — showing an even smaller range than the land stations."})

# ==================================================================
# SECTION 3: PRECIPITATION
# ==================================================================

upsc.append({"q":"Which one of the following statements about equatorial precipitation is NOT correct?","o":["A distinct dry season, similar to that of the Savanna or Tropical Monsoon climates, is a defining feature of the equatorial climate","Precipitation is heavy, generally between 1,524 mm and 2,540 mm (60 to 100 inches), and well distributed throughout the year","There are two periods of maximum rainfall, occurring in April and October, shortly after the equinoxes","Least rain generally falls at the June and December solstices"],"a":0,"e":"This statement is incorrect and reverses the actual defining feature — the text explicitly states that a distinct dry season, unlike in the Savanna or Tropical Monsoon climates, is ABSENT in the equatorial climate; there is no month without rain. The other three statements are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about local departures from the simple double-rainfall-maxima pattern in equatorial regions, per Goh Cheng Leong:\n1. Kota Bharu in Kelantan receives most of its rainfall from the North-East Monsoon towards the end of the year.\n2. Rangoon in Burma receives most of its rainfall from the South-West Monsoon between June and September.\n3. Further north and south of the equator, particularly in coastal districts open to the Trades, rainfall tends to shift towards a monsoonal pattern with heaviest rainfall in the local winter months.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 3 is incorrect — the text states the heaviest rainfall in such transitional areas comes in the local SUMMER months (June-August in the north, December-February in the south), not winter. Statements 1 and 2 are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about the mechanism of equatorial rainfall:\n1. Great heat produces much evaporation, setting up convectional air currents that lead to heavy afternoon downpours from towering cumulonimbus clouds.\n2. Mountainous regions within the equatorial belt also experience significant orographic (relief) rainfall in addition to convectional rain.\n3. Intermittent showers can also arise from cyclonic atmospheric disturbances caused by the convergence of air currents in the Doldrums.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1, 2 and 3","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only"],"a":0,"e":"All three statements are correct, matching the text's description of convectional, orographic, and cyclonic/convergence-related rainfall mechanisms operating within the equatorial climate."})

# ==================================================================
# SECTION 4: NATURAL VEGETATION
# ==================================================================

upsc.append({"q":"Which one of the following statements about the equatorial tropical rain forest is NOT correct?","o":["Unlike the savanna, Mediterranean and temperate climates, the growing season is restricted to specific months by seasonal drought or cold","In the Amazon lowlands, the dense forest is termed 'selvas'","The forest displays a distinct three-layer arrangement, with the tallest trees attaining heights of up to 45 metres","Undergrowth is generally sparse because the taller trees cut off most of the sunlight reaching the ground"],"a":0,"e":"This statement is incorrect and reverses the actual point — the text explicitly states that, UNLIKE the savanna, Mediterranean and temperate climates, the equatorial growing season is NOT restricted by drought or cold; plant growth is continuous throughout the year. The other three statements are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about the equatorial rain forest's species composition, per Goh Cheng Leong:\n1. Unlike temperate forests, where only a few species occur in a particular area, tropical rain forest trees are not found in pure stands of a single species.\n2. It has been estimated that Malaysian forests may contain as many as 490 species of trees in a single hectare.\n3. Because tropical hardwoods generally float readily on water, commercial timber haulage from these forests is comparatively easy and inexpensive.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 3 is incorrect and reverses the actual point — many tropical hardwoods do NOT float readily on water, making haulage an expensive matter, which is exactly why many tropical countries end up as net timber importers. Statements 1 and 2 are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"In the context of the equatorial rain forest, what is 'belukar', as described by Goh Cheng Leong?","o":["A less luxuriant secondary forest, characterised by short trees and very dense undergrowth, that springs up on abandoned forest clearings in Malaysia","A term for the thick, continuous canopy of foliage seen from the air over the Amazon lowlands","A local name for the mangrove forests found in coastal and brackish swamp areas","A term used for climbing plants such as lianas or rattan that grow to hundreds of metres in length"],"a":0,"e":"'Belukar' is the Malaysian term for the less luxuriant secondary forest, with short trees and dense undergrowth, that develops on abandoned forest clearings after lumbering or shifting cultivation — not the canopy, mangrove forest, or climbing plants (which are separately described in the text)."})

