upsc.append({"q":"Which one of the following statements about factors affecting the development of equatorial regions is NOT correct?","o":["Tropical soils, even in their virgin, untouched state, are inherently poor and infertile due to a complete absence of humus formation","Excessive heat and high humidity subject people to physical and mental handicaps, sunstroke, and diseases such as malaria and yellow fever","The hot, wet climate that stimulates plant growth also encourages the rapid spread of insects, pests, germs and bacteria","Livestock farming is handicapped by an absence of nutritious meadow grass, and cattle in Africa may be attacked by tsetse flies causing the disease ngana"],"a":0,"e":"This statement is incorrect and reverses the text's actual point — in its VIRGIN, untouched state, heavy leaf-fall and bacterial decomposition create a thick mantle of humus that makes tropical soil 'fairly fertile'; the RAPID DETERIORATION occurs only once this humus is used up and the vegetative cover is removed, exposing the soil to leaching by torrential rain. The other three statements are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"According to Goh Cheng Leong, which of the following is cited as an exception to the general rule of rapid tropical soil deterioration, owing to its rich volcanic ashes and the energetic efforts of its local people?","o":["The Indonesian island of Java","The Amazon basin of Brazil","The Zaire (Congo) basin","The island of Borneo"],"a":0,"e":"The text explicitly cites Java as an exception to rapid tropical soil deterioration because of its rich volcanic ashes combined with the energetic local population's systematic development efforts — not the Amazon basin, Zaire basin, or Borneo, which are instead cited as areas still lacking modern communication lines."})

